## Tasks

### Stage One: Setup and Preparation

1. Project Setup: Structure the basic project skeleton, with no real functionality yet.

### Stage Two: Agent Development

No web UI. Call agents from the CLI with a relative file path.

At this stage: Gemini inference only. All agent work goes through Gemini.

1. Build Metadata Agent: given a single file path, extract the picture's metadata and return it to state.
2. Build Vision Agent: given a single file path, extract one main tag and one secondary tag. (Initial phase only; later we feed existing tags first.) All tags must be lowercase and singular, except words that are naturally capitalized (e.g. country names, acronyms).
3. Build Storage Agent: given a file path, metadata, and a list of tags:
   3a. Use the year from metadata to move the file to `Photo_Tagged/<year>/original_file_name`. If year cannot be determined, move to `Photo_Tagged/Unknown/original_file_name`.
   3b. Return and print a dictionary of metadata, new file path, and tags. No DB writes at this step.
   3c. Also add the numeric year as a tag (e.g. `"2024"`, `"2025"` — not the word `"year"`). Year comes from Metadata Agent only; never from Vision Agent. Year tags exist so photos are searchable/filterable by year in the GUI.
4. Build Orchestrator Agent: root agent for a supplied file path.
   4a. Coordinate Metadata, Vision, and Storage agents; return success or fail.
   4b. If processing fails, return a failure log (DB write comes in a later stage).

5. Build Runner that wrap all agent

### Stage Three: Multi Directories and Multi File Processing

1. Build a facilitator class (or agent — TBD): accept a relative path.
   1a. If the path is a single file, call the Orchestrator and process it.
   1b. If the path is a directory, DFS to collect all image paths (optionally in chunks).
   1c. Loop through each image and call the Orchestrator.
   1d. After each chunk, repeat 1b–1c until no images remain in the target directory.
   1e. Path must stay within the app root (e.g. on USB, do not process paths outside the USB).
   1f. Path must not be `Photo_Tagged/` (that folder is the output of processing).

2. (Optional) Multi-thread or multi-process so multiple chunks can run in parallel.

### Stage Four: Database Integration

1. Database ERD + DDL: tables for image info (file path, metadata, tags); failure-log table for processed-but-untagged files (for human follow-up); tags as a DAG (multi-parent allowed); include migrations.
2. Migration runner: on app/web-server start, always run pending migrations.
3. Integrate DB writes into Storage Agent.
   3a. Write to DB only after the file move succeeds. Treat move + DB update as one logical unit (custom approach to minimize inconsistency risk).
4. Integrate DB writes into Orchestrator Agent.
   4a. When any sub-agent errors, Orchestrator writes the failure to the DB log.
5. Integrate existing tags with Vision Agent: before Vision runs, load all existing tags. Prefer matching existing tags; if several match, return all matched. Prefer the most specific tag only when both parent and child apply (e.g. Cat picture → tag `Cat`, not also `Animal`). This matching/exclusivity applies only to Vision tags. Year tags (Stage 2.3c) are inserted directly and skip this logic.

### Stage Five: Web Server GUI (FastAPI + React)

1. `/scan`: page to browse a file or folder path and click Scan. Agents classify and move files.
2. `/tags`: list all tags with photo counts; navigate into each tag; show DAG relationships (multi-parent / multi-child).
3. `/tags/:tag_id/pictures`: tag description, picture count, and a scrolling preview of photos tagged directly under that tag (with view options).
4. Search and filter on all pages except `/scan`.
5. `/pictures/:picture_id`: detail page with preview, metadata, tags, description; allow manual tag overwrite/edit.

